.TH CRC32 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
crc32 \- compute CRC-32 checksums
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B crc32
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B crc32
prints the CRC-32 of each
.IR file .
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
The CRC is the one used by gzip, zip, and PNG, and stored in their files:
the reflected polynomial 0xEDB88320, with an initial value and a final
exclusive or of 0xFFFFFFFF.
The CRC-32 of the 9 bytes
.B 123456789
is
.BR cbf43926 .
.PP
Each output line is the CRC as 8 lowercase hex digits, a space, and the
file name as given on the command line.
With no
.I file
operands, the line is the CRC alone.
.PP
A file that cannot be opened is reported on standard error, and the
remaining files are still processed.
An error reading a file ends the command at once.
Standard input is closed once it has been read, so naming it a second
time is a read error.
.SH OPTIONS
.B crc32
takes no options other than
.BR \-\-help .
Any other argument that begins with
.BR \- ,
except
.B \-
and
.BR \-\- ,
is an invalid option, even after a
.IR file ,
unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options, so that later arguments are file names.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options, so later arguments that
begin with
.B \-
are file names.
.PP
Variables that busybox-w32 reads in every applet, such as those that
control console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An option was invalid, a file could not be opened or read, or standard
output could not be written.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader has exited, and a write failed
before the end of the output.
Output is buffered, and when only the final write at exit fails, as
with short output, the status is 1.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the CRC-32 of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ crc32 hello.txt
363a3020 hello.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the CRC-32 of standard input:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf 123456789 | crc32
cbf43926
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the CRC-32 of a file in a zip archive, to compare it with the
file on disk:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ unzip \-p hello.zip hello.txt | crc32
363a3020
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the CRC-32 and size stored at the end of a gzip file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tail \-c 8 hello.txt.gz | od \-An \-tx4
 363a3020 00000006
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
There is no standard
.B crc32
command.
POSIX
.B cksum
computes a different CRC, which also covers the file length, and prints
it in decimal, so its values do not match those of
.BR crc32 .
.PP
The usage message shows
.I file
as required, but it is optional.
.PP
Files and standard input are read in binary mode, so the CRC of a file
matches its CRC on other systems.
Output lines end with LF, not CRLF.
.PP
.I file
may be a Windows path, with a drive letter and either kind of slash, or
a busybox-w32 device name such as
.I /dev/null
or
.IR /dev/stdin .
The name is printed as given, backslashes included.
.I /dev/stdin
does not close standard input, unlike
.BR \- ,
and cannot be opened after a
.B \-
operand.
A directory operand cannot be opened, and fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
On Windows 10 version 1903 or later, file names may contain any Unicode
characters, and on older systems only those of the system code page.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cksum (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR md5sum (1),
.BR unzip (1)
